x86_64-linux  Two recorded rules and nothing else: the C1 and D1 forms of special members the compiler writes - `Member`'s and `Implicit`'s constructors and destructors, `Arr`'s, `V`'s, `Del`'s and `Written`'s destructors - which clang emits only as the C2 and D2 a use asks for (docs/CONFORMANCE.md, "An inline member function is a strong symbol"); and cxx1's element loops `__cxx1_vec_new_1M` and `__cxx1_vec_del_1M`, the second the pad of a partly built array calls, against clang's inline code. Every constructor the program wrote agrees with clang.
x86_64-windows  cxx1's element loops `__cxx1_vec_new_1M` and `__cxx1_vec_del_1M` against clang's inline code, and nothing else: every constructor and destructor name agrees with clang.
arm64-darwin  the same two rules: C1 and D1 of `Member` and `V`, which clang builds only as bases here, and the element loops `___cxx1_vec_new_1M` and `___cxx1_vec_del_1M`.
